#!/usr/bin/env bash
# park-node — park one intention node to office hours via the graph write
# (tactic-graph-router-selector, unit 4). The graph lane's replacement for the
# legacy `dispatch:office-hours` label: mechanical failure dispositions
# (provision-failed, wrong-worktree, or any other environment error a node's
# provisioning/dispatch can hit) set the node's first-class `office_hours`
# field and land it on main through the `graph-commit` primitive. The
# router's eligibility gate (office_hours null) then keeps the node
# unselectable until a human clears it. A retryable mechanical state — an
# unroutable merge conflict, fix-attempt-cap exhaustion — is NOT parked
# directly on the source node; those route through
# `packages/intentionsutil/scripts/hold-node` instead (a tracked hold: a
# born-parked hold tactic plus a `blocked_by` edge on the source), so this
# script no longer sees them (tactic-mechanical-park-producers).
#
# Content is written through store.ts's readNode → writeNode (the validated
# writer — frontmatter serialized correctly, tactic bodies preserved); this
# script authors no markdown. The commit/stamp/fast-forward mechanics are
# graph-commit's. Ongoing phase/marker transition writes belong to
# tactic-graph-router-transitions, not here — this primitive covers only the
# park disposition.
#
# Fresh-origin/main invariant (tactic-park-node-fresh-main-clobber-fix): the
# read-modify-write below operates on origin/main's CURRENT content, never the
# possibly-stale local on-disk copy. This script fetches origin/main, overwrites
# the local node file with origin/main's version, THEN reads/mutates/writes it,
# and passes graph-commit a `--base` compare-and-swap token pinned to the blob
# it read. If origin/main advances again before the land, graph-commit refuses
# the write (park-node exits 1) rather than silently reverting the newer landed
# content — the bug this guard closes was a stale PR-branch worktree's small
# office_hours diff applying cleanly onto a since-advanced origin/main and
# reverting unrelated body/frontmatter changes there. A refusal is a legitimate
# failure the Stop-hook backstop treats as best-effort (markers left in place
# for a retry).
#
# Write-failure rollback (tactic-park-node-rollback-dirty-tree-blocks-tick-sync,
# mirrors clear-park's identical block): every exit path that does NOT land a
# commit restores intentions/<node-id>.md to the state this script found it
# in — from a pre-refresh copy captured below, NOT from FRESH_BLOB — because a
# FRESH_BLOB restore leaves origin/main's content behind as a local
# modification in the far-behind checkout this script routinely runs from. A
# leaked dirty node file trips graph-commit's assert_clean_outside_ids guard
# for every other unrelated node in that worktree, AND blocks dispatch-tick's
# own `git merge --ff-only` sync fleet-wide for as long as it persists.
# The restore is itself conditional on the checkout still being the one this
# script wrote: graph-commit has failure paths that commit the park locally, or
# `git reset --hard` onto a fresher origin/main, before exiting non-zero — and
# it only rewinds HEAD when its own RESTORE_HEAD=1, which it deliberately does
# NOT set for the main-checkout/clean-worktree callers here. So restore_node
# declines (with a diagnostic) when HEAD has moved or when the node file no
# longer hashes to the blob this script wrote; in those cases the pre-refresh
# bytes would be an uncommitted REVERT of a park that is already committed,
# which is the very dirty tree this rollback exists to prevent.
#
# The park-recommendation contract: a mechanical park must carry recoverable
# context — a reason AND a best-next-steps recommendation. `office_hours.
# recommendation` is a first-class schema.ts field (landed by
# tactic-office-hours-graph-entry); the caller passes the recommendation as the
# optional 3rd arg below, written verbatim to that field — omitted → null. This
# script never folds the recommendation into <reason> on the caller's behalf.
#
# --pr <n>: optional PR number to record into the node's `execution.pr` at
# park time (the session's already-resolved PR — this script stays gh-free,
# so the number must be passed in, never resolved here). <n> must be a
# non-negative integer. Merged into `execution` via spread, so it never
# clobbers `execution.branch`/`attempts`/`markers`/`strategy_fingerprint`/`fix`.
#
# Diagnosis-time pin (tactic-drain-disposition-diagnosis-cas): a batched
# office-hours drain diagnoses several parked nodes and interviews the human
# about each proposed disposition before executing any of them — minutes can
# pass between diagnosis and execution. Without a pin, this script's own
# execution-time self-refresh (the fetch + FRESH_BLOB resolution below) would
# silently absorb any fleet write that landed during that interview window,
# including one that changes what the human should have decided. The optional
# `--base` flag lets the caller supply the blob sha it diagnosed against, in
# any of three forms: a bare 40-hex blob sha, an `<id>=<sha>` pair (id must
# match this invocation's node id), or a path to a manifest file of such pairs
# (one per line, as produced by dump-node.ts's base-manifest.txt) — the line
# whose id matches this node id is selected. This pin is distinct from
# FRESH_BLOB: FRESH_BLOB is always re-read from origin/main at execution time
# regardless of --base; the pin is only compared against it. If they differ,
# the script exits 3 (`stale-diagnosis`) before any mutation — this is NOT
# graph-commit's own `--base` compare-and-swap (which auto-merges then parks
# on staleness); an exit-3 refusal here means the disposition itself may now
# be wrong and must route back to re-diagnosis, never to a park. See
# .claude/skills/ref-diagnosis-time-cas/SKILL.md for the full caller loop.
#
# Landing signal (tactic-graph-commit-landing-signal-unreliable, Unit 4):
# graph-commit's own exit code is NOT trusted as the verdict on whether the
# park landed. graph-commit can report failure over a write that actually
# reached origin/main (Direction B), or success over one that didn't. After
# the graph-commit invocation below — success or failure — this script asks
# the sibling `verify-landed` primitive directly: `verify-landed -C
# "$REPO_ROOT" --node "$NODE_ID" --jq '.office_hours != null'` (id and filter
# as separate arguments — see that script's header). Its three-valued verdict
# (landed/not-landed/unknown), not graph-commit's rc, decides park-node's own
# exit and whether the pre-park local copy is restored. The "HEAD moved
# locally" comparison inside restore_node() (above) stays as a diagnostic aid
# for choosing the correct restore target, but it is no longer the primary
# evidence that a park landed — verify-landed is.
#
# Usage: park-node [--pr <n>] [--base <blobsha>|<id>=<blobsha>|<manifest-file>] <node-id> <reason> [recommendation]
#
# Exit codes: 0 parked and landed on main, per verify-landed; 1 the write
# failed before graph-commit ever ran, OR verify-landed's post-graph-commit
# check came back not-landed or unknown (see the distinct stderr messages at
# the call site — an unknown verdict never claims a rollback happened), OR an
# unreachable/absent-node origin/main (absent-node stays exit 1 even when
# --base is supplied, since a node missing from origin/main is a hard
# cannot-park state, not a staleness condition); 2 usage error (including a
# malformed or unresolvable --base, or a non-integer --pr); 3 the supplied
# --base no longer matches origin/main (`stale-diagnosis` — the node changed
# between diagnosis and execution; re-diagnose and retry, nothing was
# written).
set -uo pipefail

SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
REPO_ROOT="$(cd "$SCRIPT_DIR/../../.." && pwd)"
INTENTIONS_DIR="$REPO_ROOT/intentions"
STORE_MODULE="$REPO_ROOT/packages/intentionsutil/src/store.js"

USAGE="usage: park-node [--pr <n>] [--base <blobsha>|<id>=<blobsha>|<manifest-file>] <node-id> <reason> [recommendation]"

# Leading-flags-only parse: consume flags while the current arg starts with
# `--`; the first non-flag argument ends flag parsing and every remaining
# argument is positional VERBATIM (no re-scan for flags — <reason> and
# <recommendation> are free text that may start with `-`).
PR_ARG=""
BASE_ARG=""
# Flag PRESENCE is tracked separately from the flag's VALUE: an explicitly
# supplied but empty base (`--base ''` / `--base=`) must be a usage error, not
# a silent degradation to an unpinned park. Gating the resolver on
# `-n "$BASE_ARG"` alone would make that case indistinguishable from omitting
# the flag, quietly dropping the compare-and-swap guarantee the caller asked
# for (.claude/skills/ref-diagnosis-time-cas/SKILL.md).
BASE_SUPPLIED=0
ARGS=()
while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
  case "$1" in
    --pr)
      shift
      if [[ $# -eq 0 ]]; then
        echo "$USAGE" >&2
        exit 2
      fi
      PR_ARG="$1"
      if [[ ! "$PR_ARG" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then
        echo "$USAGE (--pr must be a non-negative integer)" >&2
        exit 2
      fi
      shift
      ;;
    --base)
      shift
      if [[ $# -eq 0 ]]; then
        echo "$USAGE" >&2
        exit 2
      fi
      BASE_ARG="$1"
      BASE_SUPPLIED=1
      shift
      ;;
    --base=*)
      BASE_ARG="${1#--base=}"
      BASE_SUPPLIED=1
      shift
      ;;
    --*)
      echo "$USAGE" >&2
      exit 2
      ;;
    *)
      # First non-flag argument: stop flag parsing, take everything verbatim.
      ARGS=("$@")
      break
      ;;
  esac
done

NODE_ID="${ARGS[0]:-}"
REASON="${ARGS[1]:-}"
# Optional 3rd arg: the human-facing best-next-steps recommendation. Now that
# `office_hours.recommendation` is a first-class schema field (schema.ts), the
# caller passes it separately instead of folding it into <reason>; omitted → null.
RECOMMENDATION="${ARGS[2]:-}"
if [[ ${#ARGS[@]} -lt 2 || ${#ARGS[@]} -gt 3 || -z "$NODE_ID" || -z "$REASON" ]]; then
  echo "$USAGE" >&2
  exit 2
fi

# The node id is interpolated into a path, a graph-commit --base/--expect pair,
# and (via verify-landed) a landing check, so it is validated in FULL here — the
# same charset verify-landed enforces. src/store.ts's assertPathSafeId rejects
# only `/`, `\`, `.` and `..`, which leaves `@`, `#` and whitespace legal
# upstream; this is the gate that keeps such an id from reaching a downstream
# parser at all.
if [[ ! "$NODE_ID" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]]; then
  echo "park-node: malformed node id '$NODE_ID' — ids must match ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+\$" >&2
  exit 2
fi

# Resolve --base (if supplied) to a 40-hex blob sha, before any network call,
# so a malformed base is a cheap usage error rather than a mid-flight failure.
# Shared with clear-park via lib-base-pin.sh (see that file for the full
# branch-by-branch description); resolve_base_pin exits 2 itself on any usage
# error, using "park-node:" as the message prefix.
# The source is checked: this script runs under `set -uo pipefail` with no
# `-e`, so an unchecked `source` of a missing file would print an ENOENT, carry
# on past an ignored `resolve_base_pin: command not found`, do the origin/main
# fetch and refresh, and only die at the `-n "$PINNED_BASE"` compare below with
# an opaque `unbound variable` — turning the cheap pre-network usage error this
# block promises into a mid-flight failure. A missing sibling is a real case:
# every harness that clones these scripts must copy lib-base-pin.sh too.
source "$SCRIPT_DIR/lib-base-pin.sh" || {
  echo "park-node: could not source $SCRIPT_DIR/lib-base-pin.sh — the --base pin resolver is missing from this checkout; nothing was written" >&2
  exit 1
}
resolve_base_pin "park-node" "$NODE_ID"

TMPTS="$(mktemp --suffix=.mts)" \
  || { echo "park-node: could not create temp file for the parking write" >&2; exit 1; }
# TMPORIG holds the node file exactly as this script found it, captured BEFORE
# the origin/main refresh below overwrites it. Every non-landing exit path
# restores from it (restore_node), so a failed invocation leaves
# `git status --porcelain intentions/` as it was.
TMPORIG="$(mktemp)" \
  || { echo "park-node: could not create temp file for the pre-refresh node copy" >&2; exit 1; }
ORIG_EXISTED=0
if [[ -f "$INTENTIONS_DIR/$NODE_ID.md" ]]; then
  if ! cp "$INTENTIONS_DIR/$NODE_ID.md" "$TMPORIG"; then
    echo "park-node: could not capture the pre-refresh copy of intentions/$NODE_ID.md" >&2
    rm -f "$TMPTS" "$TMPORIG"
    exit 1
  fi
  ORIG_EXISTED=1
fi
# HEAD_BEFORE pins the commit the checkout was on when the pre-refresh copy was
# taken. The rollback below is only valid while HEAD has not moved: graph-commit
# has exit-1 paths that FIRST commit the park locally (or `git reset --hard
# FETCH_HEAD` onto a fresher origin/main) and only rewind when its own
# RESTORE_HEAD=1 — which ensure_intentions_only_base deliberately leaves at 0
# for the main-checkout/clean-worktree callers this script routinely runs from.
# Restoring the pre-refresh bytes over a HEAD that already contains the park
# would write an uncommitted REVERT of the park into the tree: it blocks
# dispatch-tick's `git merge --ff-only` sync fleet-wide, trips
# assert_clean_outside_ids for every other node write from that checkout, and —
# if a later writer commits intentions/ from there — silently clears the park a
# human gate had frozen. An empty HEAD_BEFORE (unborn HEAD, or git unavailable)
# disables the comparison rather than failing the rollback.
HEAD_BEFORE="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)"

# restore_node — put intentions/$NODE_ID.md back to its pre-refresh state.
# Copies to a temp sibling and mv's into place only on success, so a failed copy
# never truncates the node file to zero bytes. When the file did not exist
# before (a worktree branch predating the node), the restore returns the tree to
# clean — a delete only when the path is genuinely untracked.
#
# Both guards below detect "graph-commit has already committed or reset this
# file, so park-node's pre-refresh bytes are no longer the correct restore
# target" and decline to write, printing a diagnostic instead.
restore_node() {
  local head_now blob_now
  head_now="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null || true)"
  if [[ -n "$HEAD_BEFORE" && -n "$head_now" && "$head_now" != "$HEAD_BEFORE" ]]; then
    echo "park-node: HEAD moved from $HEAD_BEFORE to $head_now during the park — graph-commit already committed or reset intentions/$NODE_ID.md, so the pre-refresh copy is no longer the correct restore target; leaving the file as graph-commit left it (check 'git -C $REPO_ROOT log -1' and whether the park needs a retry)" >&2
    return
  fi
  # EXPECT_BLOB is only set once this script's own office_hours write has
  # landed on disk; before that there is nothing to compare against.
  if [[ -n "${EXPECT_BLOB:-}" ]]; then
    blob_now="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" hash-object -- "intentions/$NODE_ID.md" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    if [[ -n "$blob_now" && "$blob_now" != "$EXPECT_BLOB" ]]; then
      echo "park-node: intentions/$NODE_ID.md is no longer the content park-node wrote ($blob_now != $EXPECT_BLOB) — graph-commit already committed or reset it; leaving it in place rather than restoring the pre-refresh copy" >&2
      return
    fi
  fi

  if [[ "$ORIG_EXISTED" == 1 ]]; then
    if cp "$TMPORIG" "$INTENTIONS_DIR/$NODE_ID.md.rollback"; then
      mv "$INTENTIONS_DIR/$NODE_ID.md.rollback" "$INTENTIONS_DIR/$NODE_ID.md"
    else
      rm -f "$INTENTIONS_DIR/$NODE_ID.md.rollback"
      echo "park-node: CRITICAL — could not restore intentions/$NODE_ID.md from its pre-refresh copy; the working tree is left dirty and will block git merge --ff-only until cleaned" >&2
    fi
  elif git -C "$REPO_ROOT" ls-files --error-unmatch -- "intentions/$NODE_ID.md" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # Absent at capture time but TRACKED now: graph-commit reset the checkout
    # onto an origin/main where this node file exists. `rm -f` here would
    # manufacture a deletion of a live node file — one that blocks
    # `git merge --ff-only`, and that the next graph-commit naming this node
    # from this checkout would stage and land (assert_staged_safe permits
    # intentions/$NODE_ID.md), pruning the node and its park off main. Restore
    # from the index instead, which returns the tree to clean.
    if ! git -C "$REPO_ROOT" checkout -- "intentions/$NODE_ID.md"; then
      echo "park-node: CRITICAL — intentions/$NODE_ID.md is tracked but could not be restored with git checkout; the working tree is left dirty and will block git merge --ff-only until cleaned" >&2
    fi
  else
    rm -f "$INTENTIONS_DIR/$NODE_ID.md"
  fi
}

# MUTATED gates the rollback below: only restore intentions/$NODE_ID.md if this
# script actually wrote to it (the origin/main refresh below is the first such
# write). A pre-mutation failure (the fetch/blob-lookup above) must not
# trigger a restore of a file this script never touched.
MUTATED=0
trap '
  rc=$?
  if [[ "$MUTATED" == 1 && $rc -ne 0 ]]; then
    restore_node
  fi
  rm -f "$TMPTS" "$TMPORIG"
' EXIT

# Refresh the LOCAL node file from origin/main BEFORE reading it, so the
# office_hours mutation lands on top of the current landed content rather than a
# stale worktree copy (the invocation may come from a far-behind PR-branch
# worktree). An unreachable origin/main or a node absent from origin/main is
# genuine environment breakage — fail with a clear error, never fall back to the
# local copy (code-style.md).
if ! git -C "$REPO_ROOT" fetch origin main >&2; then
  echo "park-node: could not fetch origin/main to refresh $NODE_ID before parking" >&2
  exit 1
fi
if ! FRESH_BLOB="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" rev-parse "origin/main:intentions/$NODE_ID.md" 2>/dev/null)"; then
  # Absent-on-origin/main stays exit 1 UNCHANGED even when --base was supplied
  # — the pin check below never runs in this branch. This is a hard "cannot
  # park a node that is not landed" state, not a staleness condition: a
  # re-diagnosis retry loop would just spin forever rather than converge. It
  # needs a human, not a re-read.
  echo "park-node: intentions/$NODE_ID.md does not exist on origin/main — cannot park a node that is not landed" >&2
  exit 1
fi

# The pin check runs before ANY mutation — before the local-file overwrite
# below and before MUTATED is set to 1. The temp files and the EXIT trap
# already exist at this point (mktemp'd above, before the fetch), but the
# trap's restore is a no-op while MUTATED is still 0, and the node file itself
# has not been touched, so a stale pin has zero side effects to roll back.
if [[ -n "$PINNED_BASE" && "$PINNED_BASE" != "$FRESH_BLOB" ]]; then
  echo "park-node: stale-diagnosis — intentions/$NODE_ID.md on origin/main is now $FRESH_BLOB but the pinned --base is $PINNED_BASE; the node changed between diagnosis and execution. Re-read the node, re-decide the disposition, and retry with a freshly captured base. Nothing was written." >&2
  exit 3
fi

MUTATED=1
if ! git -C "$REPO_ROOT" show "origin/main:intentions/$NODE_ID.md" > "$INTENTIONS_DIR/$NODE_ID.md"; then
  echo "park-node: could not refresh intentions/$NODE_ID.md from origin/main" >&2
  exit 1
fi

SINCE="$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d)"
cat >"$TMPTS" <<'TS'
// argv: <storeModule> <intentionsDir> <since> <reason> <recommendation> <id> <pr>
const [storePath, intentionsDir, since, reason, recommendation, id, pr] = process.argv.slice(2);
const { readNode, writeNode } = await import(storePath);
const node = readNode(intentionsDir, id);
node.office_hours = { reason, since, recommendation: recommendation || null };
// Spread-merge so an existing execution.branch/attempts/markers/strategy_fingerprint/fix
// is never clobbered; only `pr` changes, and only when --pr was passed (pr !== "").
// Mirrors apply-node-transition.ts's defaultExecution fallback for a node with no
// execution record yet (schema requires branch/attempts/markers to be present).
if (pr !== "") {
  const defaultExecution = { branch: id, pr: null, attempts: {}, markers: [], strategy_fingerprint: null };
  const execution = node.execution ?? defaultExecution;
  node.execution = { ...execution, pr: Number(pr) };
}
writeNode(intentionsDir, node);
process.stderr.write(`park-node: set office_hours on ${id} (since=${since})\n`);
TS

if ! (cd "$REPO_ROOT" && npx tsx "$TMPTS" "$STORE_MODULE" "$INTENTIONS_DIR" "$SINCE" "$REASON" "$RECOMMENDATION" "$NODE_ID" "$PR_ARG" >&2); then
  echo "park-node: failed to write the office_hours record for $NODE_ID" >&2
  exit 1
fi

# EXPECT_BLOB captures the blob this script just wrote to intentions/$NODE_ID.md
# under its OWN REPO_ROOT, for graph-commit's --expect guard below. Hashing
# failure is genuine environment breakage — fail loudly rather than omitting
# --expect (code-style.md).
if ! EXPECT_BLOB="$(git -C "$REPO_ROOT" hash-object -- "intentions/$NODE_ID.md" 2>/dev/null)"; then
  echo "park-node: could not hash intentions/$NODE_ID.md after the office_hours write" >&2
  exit 1
fi

# -C "$REPO_ROOT" is mandatory, not cosmetic: this script's REPO_ROOT is derived
# from SCRIPT_DIR (its own on-disk location), and the office_hours write above
# targets "$INTENTIONS_DIR" under THAT repo, while graph-commit resolves its own
# repo from -C if given, else from cwd. Callers routinely invoke the main
# checkout's copy of this script by absolute path from inside a worktree, so
# without -C the two would resolve to DIFFERENT checkouts.
#
# --expect does NOT add a second, independent check on -C from this call site:
# EXPECT_BLOB is hashed under the same "$REPO_ROOT" that is passed as -C, so
# for these three wrappers the assertion is self-satisfying whenever -C
# resolves to the tree this script wrote. What it does buy here is narrower and
# still worth the two lines: (1) it asserts graph-commit's
# `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` resolution of -C lands on the same tree this
# script wrote to — which can differ if REPO_ROOT is not itself a git toplevel
# — and (2) it is a race guard, catching a concurrent writer that touched
# intentions/$NODE_ID.md between the hash above and graph-commit's staging.
# The wrong-repo case --expect exists for (graph-commit's header) is reached by
# callers that pass -C and the hash from different trees, not by this one.
GC_RC=0
"$SCRIPT_DIR/graph-commit" -C "$REPO_ROOT" --base "$NODE_ID=$FRESH_BLOB" --expect "$NODE_ID=$EXPECT_BLOB" -m "graph: park $NODE_ID ($REASON)" "$NODE_ID" || GC_RC=$?

# graph-commit's rc is discarded as the landing signal (see the header note).
# Ask verify-landed directly, unconditionally — including when GC_RC is 0 —
# because a reported success is exactly as untrustworthy as a reported
# failure until origin/main itself confirms it.
# The id and the predicate are SEPARATE arguments (`--node` / `--jq`), never a
# concatenated `"$NODE_ID@..."` token: node ids are not delimiter-free upstream
# (assertPathSafeId permits `@` and `#`), and an id spliced into the spec string
# became jq source — an id ending in `@true #` forged a `landed` verdict for any
# node, which here would suppress the rollback and write the terminal marker.
# NODE_ID is charset-validated above; verify-landed re-validates it in full.
VL_OUT="$("$SCRIPT_DIR/verify-landed" -C "$REPO_ROOT" --node "$NODE_ID" --jq '.office_hours != null' 2>&1)"; VL_RC=$?
echo "$VL_OUT" >&2

case "$VL_RC" in
  0)
    # Landed, per origin/main itself, regardless of graph-commit's own exit
    # code. The park is really there — restoring the pre-park local copy over
    # it (the EXIT trap's job on a non-zero rc) would silently look like an
    # undo of a landed park, so make MUTATED=0 so the trap declines.
    MUTATED=0
    ;;
  4)
    echo "park-node: office_hours park for $NODE_ID did NOT land on origin/main (graph-commit exit=$GC_RC, verify-landed verdict=not-landed)" >&2
    exit 1
    ;;
  *)
    echo "park-node: could not determine whether the office_hours park for $NODE_ID landed on origin/main (graph-commit exit=$GC_RC, verify-landed verdict=unknown) — re-read $NODE_ID from origin/main before any retry; this is NOT a confirmed rollback, only an unresolved check" >&2
    exit 1
    ;;
esac

# A landed park is a terminal disposition: record it so this node worker's Stop
# hook may reap the job (dispatch-self-close's node-worker invariant). Called
# unconditionally — mark-node-terminal's own ownership gate makes it a silent
# no-op when this session is parking some OTHER node (an office-hours drain).
# Best-effort: a marker write must never turn a landed park into a failure.
# This also covers the Stop-hook backstop, which calls park-node
# (dispatch-stop.sh) BEFORE dispatch-self-close, so the marker exists by the
# time the gate reads it. Note the hook `rm -f`s office-hours-reason on success
# — which is exactly why park evidence must be a separate marker.
"$SCRIPT_DIR/mark-node-terminal" "$NODE_ID" park >/dev/null 2>&1 || true

exit 0
